# Ad-Hoc Message Loop — Definition

The turn graph for `type: ticket` things: an overwritable thing per
conversation whose `status` is the addressee and whose body is the current
message (`standing-watch.md` → The Ticket). Declare `ticket` in
`_schema.yaml` with these actor names as its statuses and `closed` as
terminal; then each actor arms
`mdllm watch . --role <actor> --definition ad-hoc-message-loop --exit-on-wake`.

## Stages

**[actor-a]** / **[actor-b]**
- Entry: the other actor set `status` to this name and published.
- Performer: the named actor — the only writer while the ticket is at its
  name.
- What it produces: one reply — overwrite the body, set `status` to the next
  addressee (or `closed`), commit, publish. One message per turn: a second
  edit at the same status reaches nobody.
- Gate authority: none is conferred. A ticket carries requests, never
  approvals or rulings; the recipient acts within its own permissions and the
  irreversible goes to the human seat.

**closed (terminal)**
- Entry: whichever actor holds the turn judges the conversation finished.
- What "done" means: anything durable has been written as a proper thing and
  linked from the ticket; the ticket may be reopened later by setting
  `status` back to an actor name.

## Notes

The transcript is `git log -p` on the ticket file. Do not build another
message store. Rename the actor ids to match the roles your domain's agents
actually run as (`claude`, `codex`, `hermes`, `writer`, `reviewer` …).
